ed the first examinations and wear the robes of a shengyuan: you owe no labour service, speak to the magistrate as an equal, and teach village boys while you study for the provincial examination.","kid":"Men who passed the first exams became gentry, the leaders of their towns and villages.","rise":"Any commoner of good status could sit the examinations, but years of study, travel and gifts to examiners made a candidate the burden of his whole family, and in the Ming and Qing only about one candidate in a hundred passed the provincial examination. Merchant families could also buy degrees.","why":"The gentry were defined by law: a man who had passed at least the first level of the examinations held a degree that qualified him for office, and his wife and some relatives shared his status.","people":[]},{"n":5,"lv":4,"band":"specialists","name":"Bannermen","loc":"Qiren","term":"jakūn gūsa (the Eight Banners), irgen (commoner bannermen)","lbl":"0.6–0.75%","w":0.675,"firm":0,"pic":"/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/fukanggan.jpg","you":"You were born into a banner company and draw a stipend and grain from the state in a garrison quarter; banner courts judge you, and marrying a civilian woman can bring you punishment.","kid":"Bannermen were soldier families, many of them Manchu, who were paid by the emperor and lived in their own quarters of the cities.","rise":"Banner status was inherited. Han Chinese entered by adoption into Manchu households, often for money, and were registered apart as “separate-register” bannermen; deserters from destitute garrisons were sentenced to penal slavery or death.","why":"Every Manchu household belonged to one of the Eight Banners, which had been joined by Mongol and Han Chinese banners: in effect twenty-four armies, in companies of nominally 300 men.","people":[["fukanggan","Fuk'anggan"]]},{"n":6,"lv":5,"band":"religious","name":"Buddhist, Daoist and other clergy","term":"-","lbl":"c. 0.1–0.5% (est.)","w":0.3,"firm":0,"est":1,"pic":"/images/ency