 people, so a prosperous craftsman’s family, like any family of good status, could try to put a son through the examinations.","why":"Artisans came third in the four occupations, makers of useful things, and Qing law counted them among the good people.","people":[]},{"n":9,"lv":6,"band":"commoners","name":"Merchants","loc":"Shang","term":"","lbl":"c. 2–5% (est.)","w":3.5,"firm":0,"est":1,"pic":"/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/canton-system.jpg","you":"You are a salt merchant of Yangzhou, rich enough to patronise poets and actors, and you have paid for tutors so that your son may pass the examinations.","kid":"Merchants were placed last in old Chinese ideas, but many grew rich trading salt, tea and silk.","rise":"Merchants could buy degrees, and under the Ming and Qing their sons could sit the examinations: in 1711 the success of salt merchants’ sons at Yangzhou led to a bribery case and the chief examiner’s execution. By the late imperial period gentry and merchant families overlapped and mixed.","why":"Merchants stood last in Confucian theory, seen as a social disturbance for piling up wealth, but Qing law counted them among the good people, and many were far richer than their place suggested.","people":[]},{"n":10,"lv":7,"band":"dependent","name":"Banner bondservants","loc":"Booi","term":"booi aha (booi niyalma, men, booi hehe, women), aha","lbl":"c. 0.05–0.2% (est.)","w":0.125,"firm":0,"est":1,"pic":"/images/societies/thumbs/qing-china-10.jpg","you":"You were born into a bondservant household of one of the Upper Three Banners and serve in the Imperial Household Department, managing silk workshops in Jiangnan for the emperor’s household, which your family serves by inheritance.","kid":"Bondservant families served a banner household for generations; some farmed, while others ran the emperor’s palace business.","rise":"The status was inherited, but booi families could be moved into the Manchu banners by the emperor’s order, and Manchu families adopted bondservants’ sons